# Touch this file to run .github/workflows/make-submission.yml on this branch.

Why this exists: `workflow_dispatch` needs a token with `actions: write`, and the automation token
this repository is driven with has `contents: write` only (a dispatch attempt returns
`HTTP 403: Resource not accessible by integration`). Pushing a commit that touches this path uses
the permission the token *does* have and starts the same job with the same defaults
(`route=adopted`, `package=both`, `rebuild_payload=false`).

What the run does, and what it is for: it produces the submission `.tif` (and the `.zip` the submit
dialog accepts) on a clean `ubuntu-latest` runner from the pinned git bridge, validates every file it
emits, refuses any artifact under 100,000 bytes, re-runs the browser generator's own pipeline headless
(`scripts/check_site_generator.py`), and commits only the measurement JSON to
`data/evidence/make_submission/`. The rasters go to the run's `submission-run-<id>` artifact.

What it is *not*: it does not upload anything to DrivenData and it does not produce a score. The
score requires a human enrolment and upload — the one gate
`scripts/check_submission_readiness.py` keeps labelled `HUMAN`.

To fire it: `git commit --allow-empty -m "…" ` after editing the timestamp below, then push.

What run 36186011727 proved: with `data/bridge/manifest.json` in the tree but the `*.part-*` files it
pins absent (this repository's deliberate 128 MB patch-cap compromise), the runner took the
local-assembly branch and failed at step 7, skipping steps 8–14. Since then this workflow calls
`scripts/fetch_bridge_parts.py` first, which restores the parts from the pinned mirror commit and
verifies every sha256, so the placement step succeeds. This is the re-dispatch that proves it.

That first re-dispatch (run 36187101169) failed at the same step, because the fetcher resolved its
mirror record at `data/bridge/bridge/mirror.json` — a path that cannot exist — so it always refused
with "no mirror ref given". Fixed in PR #3, and the parts were verified against their pins out of
band: all five match, and they reassemble to 418,912,844 B / sha256 `4371c82e…`, the whole-file pin.

